techniques.summary:
Equipment bought for continuing use in the trade (lighting, audio, rigging, cases, tools, computers, a van) normally gets full relief.

Cash basis: this is the default for sole traders from 2024-25 (ITTOIA s24A; opt out by election under s25C). Capital spending is deducted when paid. The exceptions are non-depreciating assets, assets not acquired for continuing use in the trade, cars (which get capital allowances instead), land, non-qualifying intangibles, financial assets, the cost of acquiring a business, and capital spending on education or training (s33A).

Accruals basis: the annual investment allowance (AIA) gives 100% relief on up to £1,000,000 per 12-month chargeable period (CAA s51A). It applies to new or second-hand plant, including kit hired out. It is denied by the s38B general exclusions:
- spending in the period the trade permanently stops;
- cars;
- ring-fence trades;
- plant linked to a change in another person's trade where getting the AIA is a main benefit;
- the s13/13A/14 cases, such as personal kit later brought into the trade.
Above the AIA, new and unused main-rate plant (not cars) bought from 1 January 2026 gets a 40% first-year allowance (s45U, inserted by FA 2026 s29). Sole traders can claim it. Kit hired out qualifies only if s46(4B) is met, and s45V and the s46 exclusions apply. The rest of the cost goes into the main pool, which gets a writing-down allowance (WDA): 18% before 6 April 2026, and 14% for chargeable periods beginning on or after 6 April 2026 (FA 2026 s28), with a hybrid rate for periods spanning that date. The special rate is 6%.

Proposed, not law: Budget 2026 (28 Oct 2026) proposes a 50% first-year allowance for spending from 1 January 2027 and a 12% WDA from 6 April 2027 for income tax.

Kit hired out in the course of the trade is part of the trade, not 'special leasing' (s19). Money from selling kit is taxable: a receipt under the cash basis (s96A), or a disposal value that can create a balancing charge under capital allowances.

techniques.how_to_estimate_saving:
Cash basis: deduction = business share of the cost, in the tax year it is paid. Saving = deduction x marginal rate (income tax + Class 4). Rest-of-UK marginal rates for 2026-27:
- profits £12,570 to £50,270: 26%;
- £50,270 to £100,000 with no Child Benefit charge: 42%;
- £60,000 to £80,000 with one or two children: about 49% to 54% (HICBC);
- £100,000 to £125,140 (allowance taper): 62%;
- above £125,140: 47%.
Accruals: AIA on up to £1m of cost in the period; AIA spending is pooled and written off in the same period (CAA s58(4A)). On any excess that is qualifying new main-rate plant, a 40% first-year allowance (50% proposed from 1 Jan 2027) in the period of purchase. The other 60% (50%) is not put into the pool in that period, so it gets no WDA until the next period (s58(5)). Other excess spending, such as second-hand kit, goes into the main pool in the period of purchase and gets WDA at 14% or the hybrid rate (12% proposed from 6 Apr 2027). A pool of £1,000 or less can be written off (s56A). Bringing a needed purchase forward before the period end moves the relief into the earlier year. It does not change the total relief unless the marginal rate differs between the years.

funding.who:
Individuals who meet all of these:
- aged 18 or over, UK resident, with the right to work in the UK;
- starting a UK-based business, or trading for up to 60 months (from 6 April 2026; 36 months before);
- unable to get finance elsewhere (self-declaration is accepted);
- able to pass credit and affordability checks;
- holding an equity stake and a controlling interest in the business. Where there are several business partners, the applicants together must hold 50%.
Not available while bankrupt or filing for bankruptcy, on a Debt Relief Order, with an outstanding IVA or trust deed, or on a debt management plan or DAS. Some visas are excluded.

funding.conditions:
Application needs a business plan, cash flow forecast and Personal Survival Budget, plus credit and fraud checks.
- Excluded loan purposes: debt repayment; training, qualifications or education; investments that are not part of an ongoing business.
- Excluded business types include FCA-regulated activities, property investment, gambling, charities, and agents for third parties where the third party earns most of the revenue or the applicant only earns commission.
- One business per individual.
- A declined applicant must wait at least 6 months to reapply.
- Anyone on a visa must repay the loan in full at least 6 months before the visa expires.
Second Loan:
- the first loan was drawn at least 6 months ago;
- the business has traded for 3 to 60 months;
- all repayments were on schedule for the 3 months before applying (Second Loans page);
- total outstanding borrowing is £25,000 or less;
- it is treated as a new application at the rate in force then.

funding.who:
UK businesses of any legal form, including sole traders, partnerships, LLPs and companies, that meet all of these:
- trade in the UK with the core of their operations there;
- get more than 50% of turnover from trading;
- have group turnover up to £54m (£45m until lenders adopted the July 2026 expansion);
- are viable in the lender's view and not in difficulty or insolvency proceedings.
Available only through an accredited lender, and only if that lender cannot offer a facility on equivalent or better terms without the guarantee.

figures.2026-27:
6 April 2026 until lenders adopted the new terms (about end of October or start of November 2026): £45m turnover limit and 6-year maximum term.
The expansion was announced on 12 July 2026 (BBB) and 13 July 2026 (HM Treasury, ahead of Mansion House):
- turnover limit up to £54m;
- up to 10 years for term loans and asset finance up to £1.1m (NI £550,000);
- £6.5bn of extra market lending supported 'over the next four years' (BBB), or 'across this Parliament' (Budget 2026 scorecard line 75). This is lending unlocked, not government spending;
- GGS-supported lending to reach £3.35bn a year by 2028/29, up from £1.35bn.
BBB pages now show the new terms, adopted lender by lender, but no official start date has been published.
Budget 2026 (28 Oct 2026) raised BBB's financial capacity by £5bn to £30.6bn to deliver the £6.5bn GGS expansion.

funding.who:
Parents with a qualifying child, where the parent and any partner each expect to meet the minimum-income test or are treated as being in qualifying paid work. The exceptions include:
- the self-employed start-up exemption (reg 11);
- a person who has accepted a job offer, or is on unpaid leave and will return, during the application window before the start date (reg 9(2)-(2A));
- a person who was in qualifying paid work just before getting statutory sick pay, Maternity Allowance, or statutory maternity, paternity, adoption, parental bereavement or neonatal care pay, or before going on maternity, paternity, adoption, shared parental, statutory parental, parental bereavement or neonatal care leave (reg 12);
- a non-working partner of someone in qualifying paid work who gets Carer's Allowance or Scottish carer's assistance, incapacity benefit, severe disablement allowance, contributory ESA or incapacity NI credits, or is on carer's leave (reg 13).
A child qualifies until the last day of the week containing the 1 September after their 11th birthday, or 16th if disabled (reg 5). Neither partner may expect adjusted net income above £100,000 for the tax year (reg 15). Both partners need a National Insurance number (reg 9(1); GOV.UK).

funding.risks:
Cliff edge: if either parent expects adjusted net income above £100,000, the entitlement is lost. For a child under 3 that means all the funded hours; for a child with the universal 15 hours (or on the disadvantaged 2-year-old offer), 15 of the 30 hours a week.
Penalties:
- dishonest conduct: up to £3,000 (reg 31);
- careless false or misleading information: up to £500 (reg 32);
- careless material inaccuracy in a declaration: up to £300 (reg 33).
The income thresholds follow the NMW and change on 1 April, not 6 April. If the 3-monthly reconfirmation is missed, the code lapses. The free hours do not cover the extras providers charge for.

funding.conditions:
The need must be urgent, and leaving it unmet would significantly harm health, career or finances. It can be:
- rent;
- an interest-only mortgage payment;
- a utility or council tax bill, or related debts (with evidence);
- another pressing cost-of-living need, such as groceries;
- bankruptcy costs.

The applicant must have no recourse to income, accessible savings or statutory funding. Priority goes to the greatest need and least financial resilience, and to people facing additional personal or professional barriers.

Accessible savings do not include:
- money set aside for tax or NI on the previous tax year, or on work already completed and paid in the current year;
- pension funds;
- Junior ISAs for dependent children;
- Lifetime ISAs.

Applicants must complete the charity's budget planner and benefits finder (guidance PDF). The 'Apply for a Stop Gap Grant' section of the scheme web page says applicants will need photo ID, a CV, links to professional profiles and an overview of monthly income and outgoings. The form itself loads by JavaScript and could not be read.

funding.risks:
The grant will not pay:
- project development or production costs;
- business debts, including accountant fees and tax debt;
- HMRC fees or tax;
- credit card and loan debt, or other monthly debt repayments;
- course fees;
- 'opportunities';
- travel, unless in extreme circumstances and related to industry work;
- storage;
- repayments to family or friends;
- County Court Judgment costs;
- driving lessons;
- vet bills;
- private medical costs;
- school uniform;
- clothing or footwear;
- white goods, unless for a medical emergency;
- costs already incurred.

Pure live-event and corporate work does not count towards the 40 days. Event work counts only if it contributes to eligible broadcast, on-demand, streaming or cinema content. Misstating work history or savings would be dishonest.

funding.conditions:
The need must be urgent: without support, the applicant could not take a potentially transformative work opportunity. It must relate directly to a confirmed job opportunity, or demonstrably improve the chance of paid work, with a significant positive effect on career or finances. The applicant must have no recourse to income, accessible savings or statutory funding.

Accessible savings exclude:
- tax or NI set-asides for the previous year, or for work completed and paid this year;
- pensions;
- Junior ISAs;
- Lifetime ISAs.

Priority goes to the greatest need, least financial resilience and additional barriers.

The grant can contribute to any of these, or a mix:
- child or adult care;
- travel to events and festivals in Britain (not overseas);
- equipment upgrades;
- professional memberships or subscriptions;
- short vocational or professional development courses.

Other compelling needs may be considered. Apply before spending.

funding.risks:
The grant does not pay:
- project development or production costs;
- retrospective costs;
- business debts, including accountant fees and tax debt;
- HMRC costs, bills or arrears;
- immediate needs such as rent and bills (apply for the Stop-Gap Grant instead);
- storage;
- repayments to family or friends;
- County Court Judgment costs;
- driving lessons;
- vet bills;
- credit card or loan debt;
- monthly debt repayments.

Overseas travel is excluded. Misstating identity, work history or savings would be dishonest.

funding.who:
Individual creative or cultural practitioners, or a small group of practitioners who usually work together (one lead applicant manages the grant). Applicants must:
- be aged 18 or over;
- live in England;
- have a UK individual bank account in the exact name they apply in (business accounts are not accepted).

A sole trader applies in their individual capacity, not on behalf of their business or company. Applicants need at least 1 year's practice outside formal education, or 1 year's practice if self-taught; the practice need not be full time.

Supported disciplines: combined arts, dance, digital arts, libraries, literature, music, museums and collections, theatre and visual arts. 'Creative technicians' is listed as an example of an eligible practitioner. Fashion, narrative film and TV drama are not supported.

Excluded applicants:
- organisations;
- people who only perform work created by others, without adding creative input or taking a lead role;
- anyone with 1 successful or 2 unsuccessful DYCP applications since 9 Nov 2021. This is the Round 24 date, which moves on one round each time a new round opens; ineligible applications do not count;
- anyone awaiting a decision on any other ACE application or expression of interest, including National Lottery Project Grants.

funding.amount:
£2,000 to £12,000 for development activity lasting up to 1 year. The budget can include:
- a fee for the applicant's own time (ACE asks for this);
- mentoring, training, and research and development time;
- networking;
- travel, including international travel;
- collaborators;
- overheads directly related to the activity;
- personal access costs and caring costs.

Equipment can be included with competitive quotes, but buying assets is not the main purpose. Payment is usually 90% at the start and the final 10% after the final report.

funding.conditions:
The application must show:
- a clear creative or cultural practice track record;
- a development opportunity with a realistic plan and budget;
- why now is the right time;
- the future opportunities it will lead to.

It needs an example of work and a letter of support from someone involved in the applicant's career. ACE will not fund anything bought, ordered or contracted before its decision, or costs linked to formal education. Successful applicants must accept ACE's standard terms, submit a final report, and keep project and financial records, including invoices. ACE audits a sample of grants each year.

funding.amount:
Discretionary; no fixed, typical or maximum amount is published. Help offered:
- long-term help (a regular grant or paying some bills) when ongoing illness or injury stops you working;
- short-term help with rent or bills, medical expenses and childcare after a sudden loss of income;
- help with the cost of mid-career training;
- counselling or therapy;
- welfare advice and signposting.
TTG's donate page gives example awards (undated; one relates to 2022): £500 paid over 2 months plus 10 counselling sessions, and £600 paid over 2 months towards rent and bills. TTG's accounts put its grant programme for calendar year 2025 at £42,973 awarded to 226 people (counted when the trustees approve an offer, and including forward commitments), about £190 each on average.

funding.conditions:
- Each scheme has its own rules and timing. Check them before spending or starting to trade. Test Valley's grant, for example, must be applied for no later than 8 weeks after starting to trade, and is paid only after 6 months' trading.
- If a council gives a grant as Minimal Financial Assistance (MFA) under the Subsidy Control Act 2022:
  - before giving it, the council must send an MFA notification stating its gross value (s37(1)-(2));
  - the business must confirm in writing that the £315,000 limit will not be exceeded, and the council may pay only after it receives that confirmation (s37(2)(c), (3));
  - when it gives the MFA, the council provides a written MFA confirmation of the amount and date (s37(4)-(5));
  - the business must keep a written record of the MFA, its date and its gross value for at least 3 years from that date (s37(6)-(7)).
- The £315,000 limit counts all minimal or SPEI financial assistance the business has received in the current financial year so far and the two financial years before it (s36(1)-(2)).

calculator_notes.note:
- Saving = interest x business-use share x (trader's marginal income tax rate + Class 4 rate) - the lender's tax on the interest.
- Lender's tax, in order:
  - personal allowance left after their other income (from 2027-28, ITA s25(3A) sets it against non-savings income first);
  - starting-rate band = max(0, 5,000 - max(0, other non-savings income - personal allowance));
  - PSA by the lender's top band (£1,000 / £500 / £0);
  - the rest at savings rates for the year: 20/40/45% to 2026-27; 22/42/47% for 2027-28 under current law, or 24/44/49% with an 'announced' flag.
- If the lender's tax is above nil, tell the user that the lender must notify HMRC by 5 October after the tax year (TMA s7) unless they already file a return.
- A sole trader pays the interest gross: model no deduction at source unless the lender usually lives abroad.
- In a company scenario:
  - the company deducts tax from yearly interest at 20% to 2026-27, and at the savings basic rate from 2027-28 (22% law; 24% announced);
  - the lender gets credit for the tax deducted;
  - the interest is a corporation tax deduction under the loan relationship rules (not ITTOIA s34), and saves no Class 4.
- No cash-basis interest cap from 2024-25.
- Never model a gift-and-loan-back from the user's own money as a saving.
- Interest paid to the user's own minor child (from parental gifts) is the parent's income once it is over £100 a year.
